### 1. Comparison of GMFLOW Kernel Filtration with Classical Dynamical Systems

| Aspect                        | Classical Continuous Dynamical Systems                  | GMFLOW Kernel Filtration (Finite Deterministic Maps) |
|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| State Space                   | Continuous manifold (e.g., \(\mathbb{R}^d\), torus)    | Finite discrete set \(\Omega\) with \(|\Omega|=N\) |
| Evolution                     | Flow \(\phi_t\) or map \(f\)                            | Iterative map \(T: \Omega \to \Omega\)             |
| Key Object                    | Orbits, attractors, invariant measures                  | Kernel partitions \(\Pi_n\)                        |
| Information Measure           | Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy, topological entropy           | Shannon entropy \(H_n\) of partitions              |
| Rate of Divergence            | Lyapunov exponents (positive = chaos)                   | Information loss rate (negative, contraction)      |
| Bottleneck / Criticality      | Phase transitions, homoclinic tangles, crises           | Entropy Bottleneck (sharp change in \(R_n\) or \(M_n\)) |
| Stability Analysis            | Linearization, stable/unstable manifolds                | Preconditioner chains + merge hypergraph           |
| Typical Goal                  | Chaos quantification, ergodicity                        | Quantifying irreversible information collapse      |

**Key Insight**:  
GMFLOW is a **finite, combinatorial analogue** of dynamical systems theory. Instead of geometric expansion/contraction (Lyapunov exponents), we measure **information contraction** through successive coarsening of partitions. The Entropy Bottleneck plays a role similar to a “discrete phase transition” or critical slowing down in continuous systems.

